#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069797} :activity/id #uuid "f64241dc-95fe-569e-8afc-913a546e0d46" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170 :topic/id #uuid "8555552a-fb5f-5455-9150-c473e5580702" :topic/math-academy-id 381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068639 :question/id #uuid "ca60143b-d02c-5497-a134-56444304c2fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3979b4ea-910f-5037-b8e8-b0297f31c47c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499503}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cube Roots of Terms Containing Binomials"} :step/id #uuid "20d6dd44-7fc6-52a1-a076-27ddb6da9696" :step/math-academy-id 26723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069942 :tutorial/content "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e24a880-f9da-5e1e-b0bf-5bb2128d72a1" :step/math-academy-id 4295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :question/id #uuid "2ca53a08-7b11-552c-983f-96e7a392156c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "942732a6-ca14-5b61-b8fb-ab9ef81610cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551334}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Monomial"} :step/id #uuid "eea06921-8121-51a7-86ad-15824cc00550" :step/math-academy-id 4296 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :question/id #uuid "c52719a7-544b-5844-b716-9b9d2999c028" :question/math-academy-id "e-1358"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c07788-b01d-5788-b2f1-0a027ea53cb2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f7015e37-4cd2-5ffb-814e-264ca9a0b345" :step/math-academy-id 4299}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068929}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499613} :activity/id #uuid "71ec2c34-3e2e-5ae6-b69a-b5dbce78416c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170 :topic/id #uuid "8555552a-fb5f-5455-9150-c473e5580702" :topic/math-academy-id 381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205338bd-e2b8-5ef4-946e-60ea49b4acef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{8-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498802} {:answer/id #uuid "282d4dba-8d23-5113-9d73-c5f211634733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498815} {:answer/id #uuid "49dc74d2-9990-56b4-a55f-41426d3ad2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt[3]{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498902} {:answer/id #uuid "61676d8e-8c9c-5429-931f-fd3bf93c7af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498951} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a24519-9959-5f47-b1b6-7b079c96d48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499132} :answer-field/id #uuid "69253319-8b21-56a4-a375-b0e2ce7050c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "860064d1-6642-51e5-83a1-e8a0a0b4fe77" :question/math-academy-id "q-82144" :question/problem "The expression $\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "The expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}} & =8-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2966d86a-c92e-5b05-b39e-7b16e7e474c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bca481-7d34-5259-91cb-874aca82b8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498885} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0d4325-0f8d-5187-a9a0-6346e4c4c09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498914} {:answer/id #uuid "6831cc29-926d-5a0f-9b44-864c4b8510e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(1-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498964} {:answer/id #uuid "75522216-0f2f-585a-a400-b83699659bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498991} {:answer/id #uuid "8516910a-3ad3-5900-bf26-9abcee760676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8712f6-aae6-50a7-bb10-c5dc0c11f25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "49c6fb96-74c6-5438-982d-666dbe67b2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-52493" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(3(x-1))}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b51e797c-a377-52f5-acf1-019291d3535d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34df0886-f589-5a77-91fd-b574dff15d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498850} {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} {:answer/id #uuid "669efc65-a593-55a2-9cb2-e9832fd6adb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498959} {:answer/id #uuid "9d702685-480a-51e1-930a-6e01b50056fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0037796-de78-55f7-b178-e17d9506d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd8c6c1-d268-5074-8c20-054b800150f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82157" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{a}{2x})}^{3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{a}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5848270-698c-55c8-973b-062f99103d08"}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498726}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069592} :activity/id #uuid "a94cc1b0-429b-5583-ab2e-ced406c12a6a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048267 :topic/id #uuid "8dee6cdf-6bc7-52db-8873-a393c17a11e1" :topic/math-academy-id 384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070024 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{ or } \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =4-1 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+1) & =4 \\\\ -x-1 & =4 \\\\ -x-1+1 & =4+1 \\\\ -x & =5 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "35d16431-4611-59fc-8888-c1db1a7b9902" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8821 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0212403d-0f66-5be5-8ebb-0dcacbe7f08f" :step/math-academy-id 143132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070025 :tutorial/content "So, we've seen that the solutions to the equation \n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nare $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$ \n\nWe can verify that these solutions are correct by substituting them back into the original equation and checking we get a true statement:\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{blue}3}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |3+1| & =4 \\\\ |4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{red}-5}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |-5+1| & =4 \\\\ |-4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{red}-5}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}$ are both solutions to our absolute value equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "561ffe88-9340-5e0a-840a-6946f446a9f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8822 :tutorial/title "Checking the Solution"} :step/id #uuid "88ed50f9-5a31-59c8-9fca-2e1513db0b7a" :step/math-academy-id 143133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068489 :question/id #uuid "b5ef1eb1-4603-5d02-8478-94e8de89ad85" :question/math-academy-id "e-14216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c402c36-0eaa-5281-a7b3-051a3f65e3f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487370}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Absolute Value Equations"} :step/id #uuid "5413cce8-d1e6-5e3b-b122-54f31f20e10c" :step/math-academy-id 143134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068490 :question/id #uuid "9dd0ea37-1775-577e-9e48-d82a50b28963" :question/math-academy-id "e-14221"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee2e348-0af1-5e7d-a4b4-f3450ee9c19f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547009}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Absolute Value Equations"} :step/id #uuid "2e7e6f3f-5ced-591e-96c7-4cf8ac5ac6d1" :step/math-academy-id 143138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068492 :question/id #uuid "9aa175ae-420f-5bef-855b-4f0aadbac062" :question/math-academy-id "e-14225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61dbfea6-68e8-56b0-aae1-a48fdd8839b1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551343}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Absolute Value Equations With Negative Variable Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "e9eff26c-6915-5c65-8459-e5a6a20e0ba1" :step/math-academy-id 143141 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068494 :question/id #uuid "094b2695-32c7-55dd-b3e1-27afcd3a09c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ede7f82-5453-553b-b58e-31d7d9452336" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487369}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Absolute Value Equations Containing Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "6afe9233-9d29-545c-9bcc-718ed7ad41e6" :step/math-academy-id 143144}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068932}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492987} :activity/id #uuid "fdf293e8-c26a-525e-9d5d-8af56d0bc18f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048267 :topic/id #uuid "8dee6cdf-6bc7-52db-8873-a393c17a11e1" :topic/math-academy-id 384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1745440e-88bf-5d51-8897-e4414167e24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-11$ or $-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492261} {:answer/id #uuid "614a5039-7de1-5dbf-bca4-d8ffc425678e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492413} {:answer/id #uuid "76d2fb58-4ccb-56f8-910a-14c6ece3d1fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492456} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ed8913-7141-5fed-a6ed-846233cc1d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492602} {:answer/id #uuid "f02f7135-1784-56eb-88e1-1c8c91546081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492456} :answer-field/id #uuid "51aef6b7-5dcd-5253-a886-fdee125552e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b25ca648-9cf3-58f7-bad1-6cf2e59324cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154645" :question/problem "If $|5-x|=6,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|5-x|=5-x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-x| & =6 \\\\ 5-x & =6 \\\\ -x & =1 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5-x|=-(5-x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-x| & =6 \\\\ -(5-x) & =6 \\\\ -5+x & =6 \\\\ x & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=11.$"} :step/id #uuid "a1c53633-2063-5148-ab3a-e83f6501d22e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0701c6fb-b8b0-5030-bb54-6715655e1d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{2}{7}$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492232} {:answer/id #uuid "591e63f9-b0da-50e5-9f5f-c6dc29186793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $-\\frac{2}{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492392} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3a1f6d-cc83-5713-9c86-11f797705b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{7}$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492500} {:answer/id #uuid "a4bff5c8-756d-5c90-a938-61faf60d9476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492550} {:answer/id #uuid "b716a5c1-2bda-5fe2-a26b-d8a2524741b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ or $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492586} :answer-field/id #uuid "dac4219c-b92c-5d85-9814-6729d48e7233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf001cda-9f10-546a-97c2-0d9313d65e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-15254" :question/problem "If $|11-4x|=3,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|11-4x|=11-4x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|11-4x| & =3 \\\\ 11-4x & =3 \\\\ -4x & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{-4x}{-4} & =\\frac{-8}{-4} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|11-4x|=-(11-4x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|11-4x| & =3 \\\\ -(11-4x) & =3 \\\\ -11+4x & =3 \\\\ 4x & =14 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & =\\frac{14}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b16ab395-e781-5b4b-970d-5cc8a7610c9e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "589f4d35-5413-5d87-83c1-4a59ecc9ebd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{9}{5}$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492388} {:answer/id #uuid "9d463002-439b-562b-9798-0df675319b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492523} {:answer/id #uuid "b557add2-f5fc-5a0c-ad04-8a1c5f8ba3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $\\frac{9}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492580} {:answer/id #uuid "cc661ccb-9cb1-5767-b7fa-3f7b29153ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492623} {:answer/id #uuid "e68b697d-110c-5bd9-88a6-6ccd86dd44e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $-\\frac{9}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492388} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffecce80-4a83-596b-bd4c-c3a2d3d4cfc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc058aee-cd4e-56a4-b735-dc759e998e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15257" :question/problem "If $|5x-12|=3,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|5x-12|=5x-12,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x-12| & =3 \\\\ 5x-12 & =3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5x-12|=-(5x-12),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x-12| & =3 \\\\ -(5x-12) & =3 \\\\ -5x+12 & =3 \\\\ -5x & =-9 \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5} & =\\frac{-9}{-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{9}{5}$ and $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e83a3e23-66a2-56f7-b2eb-d3f3cc24ff4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492975}}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492222}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356} :activity/id #uuid "b830a80c-d680-54e0-801a-894c774ffea4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302 :topic/id #uuid "92b2c666-a4c0-5f99-83f5-ccf8e995ac64" :topic/math-academy-id 2471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148684 :tutorial/content "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed1b9eba-931a-5908-99c6-58b738afef7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1937 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "642a7826-566a-5e56-b810-cb6fb7d5fa0c" :step/math-academy-id 531824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147198 :question/id #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050886}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489253}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"} :step/id #uuid "c0ba8192-ba62-514c-b119-d4b80274d19c" :step/math-academy-id 531825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148683 :tutorial/content "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65e48fd4-d6c3-5e02-aad1-9a8dae46b0bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1936 :tutorial/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "4ad684e1-3cee-5aab-8d3d-e3b084e25e37" :step/math-academy-id 531828 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147199 :question/id #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" :question/math-academy-id "e-1988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500378}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"} :step/id #uuid "c9b07cd7-55e0-5b22-8e5e-94373891336a" :step/math-academy-id 531829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147215 :question/id #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551241}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "5e381067-3801-5773-b9a5-d5c897aa20dc" :step/math-academy-id 531832}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147671}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500423} :activity/id #uuid "ec03d21f-b720-5639-be54-4e3700ddbedb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302 :topic/id #uuid "92b2c666-a4c0-5f99-83f5-ccf8e995ac64" :topic/math-academy-id 2471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421513ab-7ba9-53ad-8cc4-202843fff382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499779} {:answer/id #uuid "49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499793} {:answer/id #uuid "77ce4af4-fcc6-55af-ac0a-de8056e6b319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (3.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499875} {:answer/id #uuid "961d3d8b-abcf-51c0-a2d4-4c9ea00eafc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (0.9)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499947} {:answer/id #uuid "fffde48d-2021-53c1-b6ab-24b4f090abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (19)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499793} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" :question/math-academy-id "q-318202" :question/problem "When a new article was first published on a news website, it had $40$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after publication, the number of views increased by $190%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the article was published, and $t\\le 6?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.9$ is the growth rate ($190%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after publication\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=40\\cdot (1+1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "450,792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499699} {:answer/id #uuid "143d216d-e1b6-5b77-b80e-11048d8298ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "79,379" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499700} {:answer/id #uuid "6de7a497-dd0c-5d56-ac16-d0eaf3570086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64,467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499860} {:answer/id #uuid "96687e47-684e-58fb-863d-2da17bbf8bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59,058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499948} {:answer/id #uuid "cec9699d-a223-50f9-941c-c2542b406244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "441,156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499699} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a2769d-116f-58e7-ae7f-aaabbc05f186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-1416" :question/problem "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people."} :step/id #uuid "90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500444}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6885483a-aa71-5fea-be44-2ce9914f562d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499854} {:answer/id #uuid "99d3b702-8f26-5fec-864d-fc1d46d65b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499952} {:answer/id #uuid "a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "532" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499977} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6b2e34-5b0d-511b-9c71-0da9a7de6e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499993} {:answer/id #uuid "be53aed4-2603-538a-bb13-6ffecb59a0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499977} :answer-field/id #uuid "1232ee90-f900-5499-a331-ceb50d527115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-317613" :question/problem "A video was posted on a streaming platform with $50$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the video was posted, the number of views increased by $120%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=50$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after the video was posted\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=50\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{3}≈532\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $532.$"} :step/id #uuid "99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$63$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499771} {:answer/id #uuid "529fdd70-21b7-5a61-acff-82b1d5ee51b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$65$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499810} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2a7572-9dfa-5124-bbbe-d5a09f71180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$68$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499925} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf143da-90ae-5cc9-9f95-1d1c3df1b0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$54$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499927} {:answer/id #uuid "b5201925-5100-5ac3-b4cc-f62281b63503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$58$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499771} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61916" :question/problem "A watch collector bought a watch for $$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $$63.$"} :step/id #uuid "d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500427}}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499655}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065236} :activity/id #uuid "ee2f5838-c8a6-5332-932d-ba9cbf1432c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048928 :topic/id #uuid "ccd0b2ba-3714-5783-a6c4-70021ded19c3" :topic/math-academy-id 370} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065342 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to factor the difference of two squared monomials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to factor the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4\n$$\n\nThis expression is a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can factor the expression using the difference of squares formula with $a=x$ and $b=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f50b677-50fc-5584-82de-7ad393d19d72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7189e486-f5a6-5ce7-9803-33bcfde7853b" :step/math-academy-id 4247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064328 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063963 :question/id #uuid "f1636f93-d678-581d-85a7-0ff